{% extends 'layouts/base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}SWAT - FAQ{% endblock %}

{% block body %}

    <main>

        <section class="faq-page">

            <div class="hero">
                <h1 class="hero_title">FAQ</h1>
                <p class="hero_subtitle">
                    Find answers to common questions about SWAT, its features, and how to get started.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">General Questions</span>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-list">

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        What is SWAT?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        SWAT is a lightweight toolkit designed to automate Symfony deployments with GitHub Actions,
                        a minimal frontend stack, and a simple server-based deployment workflow.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Who is SWAT for?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        SWAT is built for developers who want a clean and efficient Symfony setup without relying
                        on heavy frontend frameworks or overly complex infrastructure.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Is SWAT production-ready?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT is designed to support real deployment workflows with preproduction and production
                        environments, automated GitHub Actions pipelines, and server-side environment configuration.
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Installation &amp; Setup</span>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-list">

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Do I need Symfony CLI to use SWAT?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        No. Symfony CLI is optional but recommended. You can run the project with symfony serve
                        if you have it installed, or use PHP's built-in server instead.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Do I need Docker to use SWAT?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        No. SWAT does not require Docker. It is designed to work with a standard Symfony setup,
                        local PHP tooling, and SSH-based deployments.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Can I use yarn instead of npm?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT works with both npm and yarn. Use the package manager that best fits your workflow.
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Deployment &amp; CI/CD</span>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-list">

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        How does SWAT handle deployments?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        SWAT uses GitHub Actions to build, validate and deploy the project automatically.
                        Deployments are performed over SSH using environment-specific secrets.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Does SWAT support multiple environments?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT is designed to support at least preproduction and production environments,
                        with separate GitHub secrets and deployment targets for each one.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Why do I need GitHub secrets?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        GitHub secrets are used to securely store sensitive deployment information such as
                        SSH keys, server hostnames, ports, users, and deployment paths.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Can I customize the GitHub Actions workflow?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT includes a ready-to-use GitHub Actions workflow, but you are free to adapt it
                        to your own development and deployment strategy.

                        <br><br>

                        For example, you may want to add additional tests, integrate code quality tools,
                        change the deployment logic, add new environments, or adjust the build process.

                        <br><br>

                        If you modify the workflow, make sure that your GitHub secrets, server configuration
                        and project dependencies remain consistent with the updated pipeline.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Can I add or remove Symfony, Composer or npm packages?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT is designed to be a flexible starting point for your own Symfony projects.
                        You are free to add or remove Symfony bundles, Composer dependencies or npm packages
                        depending on your application's needs.

                        <br><br>

                        However, keep in mind that SWAT includes a predefined CI/CD pipeline using GitHub Actions.
                        Some steps in the pipeline rely on specific tools or packages.

                        <br><br>

                        For example, removing phpunit or other development tools may cause the CI
                        pipeline to fail if the corresponding GitHub Actions step still expects them to be present.

                        <br><br>

                        If you significantly change the project's dependencies, you may need to update the
                        GitHub Actions workflow to match your new setup.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Do I need to use the same GitHub branch structure?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        No. SWAT uses a simple branch structure with dev and master by default,
                        but you are free to adapt it to your own workflow.

                        <br><br>

                        The default pipeline assumes that dev is used for preproduction deployments
                        and master for production deployments. If you use a different branch strategy
                        such as main, staging or release branches, you may need to update the
                        GitHub Actions workflow accordingly.

                        <br><br>

                        As long as your CI pipeline and branch protection rules remain consistent
                        with your deployment strategy, SWAT can work with any branch structure.
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Configuration &amp; Troubleshooting</span>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-list">

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        What is TRUSTED_PROXIES used for?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        TRUSTED_PROXIES is only required if your application runs behind a reverse proxy
                        such as Nginx, Traefik or a load balancer. It allows Symfony to correctly detect the client IP
                        and request information. If your server is directly exposed to the internet, this variable
                        should not be defined.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Why are my routes not working correctly?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        SWAT includes a default .htaccess file to ensure proper URL rewriting on Apache servers.
                        If your application runs on Apache, this file allows Symfony to correctly redirect all requests
                        to public/index.php.

                        <br><br>

                        However, some environments such as Nginx or custom reverse proxy setups do not use
                        .htaccess files. In those cases, the file may be unnecessary or interfere with
                        the server configuration.

                        <br><br>

                        If you encounter routing issues:
                        <br>
                        • Make sure your server correctly rewrites requests to public/index.php
                        <br>
                        • Remove the .htaccess file if your environment does not use Apache
                        <br>
                        • Ensure your web server root points to the public/ directory
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        What should I check if a deployment fails?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Start by checking your GitHub Actions logs, GitHub secrets, SSH connectivity,
                        server permissions, deployment path and environment variables on the target server.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-item">
                    <button class="faq-item_question" type="button">
                        Can I adapt SWAT to my own infrastructure?
                    </button>

                    <div class="faq-item_answer">
                        Yes. SWAT is intentionally lightweight and can be adapted to different server setups,
                        deployment strategies and project structures, as long as the CI/CD workflow remains consistent.
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="section-title">
                <span class="section-title_text">Need more help?</span>
            </div>

            <div class="next-steps">

                <p class="next-steps_text">
                    Looking for more details? Explore the <strong>documentation</strong> or return to the
                    <strong>setup guide</strong> to review the installation steps.
                </p>

                <div class="next-steps_actions">

                    <a href="{{ path('app_documentation') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                        Documentation
                    </a>

                    <a href="{{ path('app_setup_guide') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                        Setup Guide
                    </a>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>

    </main>

{% endblock %}